item+ struct src/phext.rs :: Chain :: pub struct Chain { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @struct Chain  A chain: an ordered series of complete phexts within one stream, joined by CHAIN_BREAK (0x02) and optionally terminated by CHAIN_CLOSE (0x03). The chain layer is a stream-level layer, one rung above the library break: the nine dimension breaks structure a single phext, while the two chain bytes structure a stream of phexts. Each member is a whole phext, worked with the ordinary phext functions in this module; read in succession, a chain's members are editions in sequence — the chain is how a phext stream encodes time.  `closed` records how the chain ended: true when a CHAIN_CLOSE terminated it, false when the stream simply ran out (EOF). A chain that ends at EOF is unclosed — possibly truncated — and is never silently promoted to closed. A stream with no chain bytes at all is a single unclosed chain holding one member phext: every pre-0.6 phext reads this way. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1207 :: 1
item+ struct src/phext.rs :: Coordinate :: pub struct Coordinate { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @struct Coordinate  provides access to a default-initialized coordinate at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1  phext coordinates are formed along a 9-dimensional hierarchy with three main arms of the form z3.z2.z1/y3.y2.y1.x3.x2.x1 where:  Z - this arm contains the library (z3), shelf (z2), and series (z1) dimensions Y - this arm contains the collection (y3), volume (y2), and book (y1) dimensions X - this arm contains the chapter (x3), section (x2), and scroll (x1) dimensions  Dense graphs: phext is sparse by design. Coordinate components are unbounded, but a fully-populated dense graph contains the product of its dimensions' extents in scrolls — a dense block spanning 1000 in each of 9 dimensions implies 10^27 scrolls. Materializing, exploding, or indexing such a graph costs memory proportional to its populated scroll count. The old 999 cap never protected against this (999^9 was already unreachable); sparsity does. Keep graphs sparse. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 264 :: 1
