item+ function src/phext.rs :: spans :: pub fn spans(input: &str) -> Vec<ScrollSpan> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn spans  Builds a borrowed span index over `input`: one `ScrollSpan` per scroll, in document order, giving each scroll's coordinate and the byte range of its text in `input`.  The walk is the `phokenize` walk — the same dimension breaks and the same coordinate arithmetic — except that it records byte ranges instead of allocating a `String` for every scroll. For every returned span, `&input[span.range]` is exactly the scroll `explode` returns at `span.coord`, and `span.range.start` is the offset `offset` reports for that coordinate.  Empty-scroll contract: a scroll exists iff it holds at least one byte — the definition `explode` encodes — so `spans` reports only non-empty scrolls. Delimiter runs that encode empty scrolls (leading breaks, runs between distant scrolls, trailing breaks) produce no span; an empty document, or a document of only delimiters, produces no spans. `phokenize` remains the tokenizer of record for empty slots: it additionally reports a final empty positioned scroll in those trailing cases, where `spans` reports none.  Chain bytes (0x02 / 0x03) are not dimension breaks, so — exactly as in `phokenize` and `explode` — they are scroll content here. This index covers a single phext (one universe); chain structure is the `parse_chains` layer above it.  Motivating consumer: holo-phext, whose `src/index.rs` carries a 323-line re-implementation of this walk, plus a duplicated private dimension-break predicate in its core, because libphext exported only owned-`String` tokenization (`phokenize`/`explode`) and a single-scroll `offset`. Downstream stores should index with `spans` instead of re-walking the format.  # Example  ``` use libphext::phext::{spans, to_coordinate}; let input = "alpha\u{17}beta"; let index = spans(input); assert_eq!(index.len(), 2); assert_eq!(index[0].coord, to_coordinate("1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1")); assert_eq!(&input[index[0].range.clone()], "alpha"); assert_eq!(&input[index[1].range.clone()], "beta"); ``` ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1012 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: subtract :: pub fn subtract(left: &str, right: &str) -> String { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn subtract  Subtracts the scrolls from `left` where there is also content at those coordinates in `right`  Note: This makes it easy to generate a mask for a given phext archive. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1243 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: to_coordinate :: pub fn to_coordinate(address: &str) -> Coordinate { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn to_coordinate  translates a phext string to a strongly-typed address  @param address  text to parse ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1407 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: to_string :: pub fn to_string(&self) -> String { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn to_string  produces a quoted string for the given phext address in canonical format (z3.z2.z1/y3.y2.y1/x3.x2.x1)  @param coord  the coordinate to translate ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1478 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: to_urlencoded :: pub fn to_urlencoded(&self) -> String { :: Function to_urlencoded takes no inputs beyond the value it is called on, and gives back String. :: signature :: 1488 :: 0
item+ function src/phext.rs :: uncheck_scroll :: pub fn uncheck_scroll(scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn uncheck_scroll  Returns the payload of a checked scroll when its trailer verifies; None for unchecked, malformed, or corrupted scrolls. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1988 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: verify_checked_scroll :: pub fn verify_checked_scroll(scroll: &str) -> bool { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn verify_checked_scroll  true when `scroll` ends with a well-formed integrity trailer whose checksum matches the payload before it. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1971 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: verify_commit :: pub fn verify_commit(input: &str, entries: &[Coordinate], commit: Coordinate) -> bool { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn verify_commit  true when the scroll at `commit` is a commit marker whose count matches `entries` and whose checksum matches the entries' current contents, fetched in order. Any missing, altered, or torn entry — or a missing marker — yields false: the batch did not commit. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2249 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: volume_break :: pub fn volume_break(&mut self) { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn volume_break ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment :: 1543 :: 1
item+ struct src/phext.rs :: Chain :: pub struct Chain { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @struct Chain  A chain: an ordered series of complete phexts within one stream, joined by CHAIN_BREAK (0x02) and optionally terminated by CHAIN_CLOSE (0x03). The chain layer is a stream-level layer, one rung above the library break: the nine dimension breaks structure a single phext, while the two chain bytes structure a stream of phexts. Each member is a whole phext, worked with the ordinary phext functions in this module; read in succession, a chain's members are editions in sequence — the chain is how a phext stream encodes time.  `closed` records how the chain ended: true when a CHAIN_CLOSE terminated it, false when the stream simply ran out (EOF). A chain that ends at EOF is unclosed — possibly truncated — and is never silently promoted to closed. A stream with no chain bytes at all is a single unclosed chain holding one member phext: every pre-0.6 phext reads this way. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1308 :: 1
