item+ function src/phext.rs :: append :: pub fn append(input: String, location: Coordinate, scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn append  Appends `scroll` at `location`, which must sort strictly after the document's last non-empty scroll. The result is the input with only a delimiter run and the new content added at its end — a host can write just that suffix to a file opened in append mode and sync once.  Returns None when the scroll is empty (empty scrolls do not exist) or `location` does not sort after the document's end. Trailing delimiter bytes in the input encode only empty scrolls, so they are dropped first and never shift the appended scroll's address.  On None the append was rejected; the input String was consumed, so pass a clone if the caller needs it after a rejected append.  The document's end is located with `last_scroll_coordinate`, a backward delimiter scan over the bytes — existing text is never re-tokenized, so a call's cost tracks the delimiter run at the end of the document, not the size of the document. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2152 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append_batch :: pub fn append_batch(input: String, entries: &[(Coordinate, &str)]) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn append_batch  Appends several scrolls in one call. Entries must be non-empty and in strictly ascending coordinate order, the first sorting after the document's end. The whole batch is a single suffix of the result, so a host persists it with one write and one sync.  This is construction atomicity, not storage atomicity: whether the suffix lands completely is the host filesystem's business (see the durability profile in the crate docs). Pair the batch with a `commit_scroll` as its final entry to make a torn batch *detectable* at recovery — that is the part a format library can own. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2204 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append_checked :: pub fn append_checked(input: String, location: Coordinate, scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn append_checked  `append` with an integrity trailer: the stored scroll is `checked_scroll(scroll)`, verifiable later with `fetch_checked`. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2186 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: commit_scroll :: pub fn commit_scroll(entries: &[(Coordinate, &str)]) -> String { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn commit_scroll  Builds the commit marker for a batch: a small scroll binding the batch's entry count and a checksum over each entry's coordinate and content, in order. Append it (via `append_batch`) as the batch's final scroll, at a coordinate after every entry. `entries` here are the (coordinate, content) pairs exactly as appended — checked contents included, when the batch uses checked scrolls. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2235 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: fetch_checked :: pub fn fetch_checked(input: &str, target: Coordinate) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn fetch_checked  Fetches the scroll at `target` and returns its payload, but only when the scroll is checked and verifies. A missing, unchecked, or torn scroll yields None — recovery code can treat None as "not committed".  This is a single-scroll lookup: one early-exit scan, linear in the document, with no per-entry repetition inside the call. Callers that verify a whole batch should use `verify_commit`, which indexes the document once for the marker and every entry. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2009 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: verify_commit :: pub fn verify_commit(input: &str, entries: &[Coordinate], commit: Coordinate) -> bool { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn verify_commit  true when the scroll at `commit` is a commit marker whose count matches `entries` and whose checksum matches the entries' current contents, in order. Any missing, altered, or torn entry — or a missing marker — yields false: the batch did not commit.  The document is indexed with a single `explode` pass and the marker and every entry are read from that map. The previous implementation ran the scanning `fetch` once for the marker and once per entry, so verification cost grew quadratically in document length — the cost a WAL recovery pass pays over the whole log, per transaction. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2260 :: 1
