item+ function src/phext.rs :: Coordinate::validate_coordinate :: pub fn validate_coordinate(&self) -> bool { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn validate_coordinate  determines if coord points to a valid phext address  @param coord: the coordinate to reset ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1623 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append :: pub fn append(input: String, location: Coordinate, scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn append  Appends `scroll` at `location`, which must sort strictly after the document's last non-empty scroll. The result is the input with only a delimiter run and the new content added at its end — a host can write just that suffix to a file opened in append mode and sync once.  Returns None when the scroll is empty (empty scrolls do not exist) or `location` does not sort after the document's end. Trailing delimiter bytes in the input encode only empty scrolls, so they are dropped first and never shift the appended scroll's address.  On None the append was rejected; the input String was consumed, so pass a clone if the caller needs it after a rejected append.  The document's end is located with `last_scroll_coordinate`, a backward delimiter scan over the bytes — existing text is never re-tokenized, so a call's cost tracks the delimiter run at the end of the document, not the size of the document. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2317 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append_batch :: pub fn append_batch(input: String, entries: &[(Coordinate, &str)]) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn append_batch  Appends several scrolls in one call. Entries must be non-empty and in strictly ascending coordinate order, the first sorting after the document's end. The whole batch is a single suffix of the result, so a host persists it with one write and one sync.  This is construction atomicity, not storage atomicity: whether the suffix lands completely is the host filesystem's business (see the durability profile in the crate docs). Pair the batch with a `commit_scroll` as its final entry to make a torn batch *detectable* at recovery — that is the part a format library can own. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2369 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append_checked :: pub fn append_checked(input: String, location: Coordinate, scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn append_checked  `append` with an integrity trailer: the stored scroll is `checked_scroll(scroll)`, verifiable later with `fetch_checked`. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2351 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: book_break :: pub fn book_break(&mut self) { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn book_break ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment :: 1698 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: chapter_break :: pub fn chapter_break(&mut self) { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn chapter_break ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment :: 1689 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: checked_scroll :: pub fn checked_scroll(scroll: &str) -> String { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn checked_scroll  Returns `scroll` with an integrity trailer appended: one final line `phext-check:xxh3-128:<checksum of the payload>`. In a checked scroll the final line is reserved — verification always reads the LAST marker line, and the checksum binds the entire payload before it, so a marker line inside the payload cannot forge a trailer.  The checksum detects accidental corruption and torn writes. xxh3 is not a cryptographic hash: this is integrity, not authentication. An empty scroll stays empty (a scroll with no bytes does not exist, so there is nothing to check). ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2123 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: collection_break :: pub fn collection_break(&mut self) { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn collection_break ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment :: 1719 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: commit_scroll :: pub fn commit_scroll(entries: &[(Coordinate, &str)]) -> String { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn commit_scroll  Builds the commit marker for a batch: a small scroll binding the batch's entry count and a checksum over each entry's coordinate and content, in order. Append it (via `append_batch`) as the batch's final scroll, at a coordinate after every entry. `entries` here are the (coordinate, content) pairs exactly as appended — checked contents included, when the batch uses checked scrolls. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2400 :: 1
