item+ function src/phext.rs :: fetch :: pub fn fetch(input: &str, target: Coordinate) -> String { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn fetch  retrieves the scroll located at the given coordinates.  @param phext  the raw phext buffer to search @param coord  coordinate to select the scroll from ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1276 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: fetch_checked :: pub fn fetch_checked(input: &str, target: Coordinate) -> Option<String> { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn fetch_checked  Fetches the scroll at `target` and returns its payload, but only when the scroll is checked and verifies. A missing, unchecked, or torn scroll yields None — recovery code can treat None as "not committed".  This is a single-scroll lookup: one early-exit scan, linear in the document, with no per-entry repetition inside the call. Callers that verify a whole batch should use `verify_commit`, which indexes the document once for the marker and every entry. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2174 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: fetch_point :: pub fn fetch_point(&self, address: &str) -> String { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn fetch_point  fetches a point address string. A bare point is a caret: it fetches the empty string. The offset form fetches the selected characters: from the point, forward `offset` characters (scalars) within the scroll, terminating at the scroll boundary. Any parse or resolution failure fetches the empty string, as Chain::fetch does. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment :: 2018 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: fetch_range :: pub fn fetch_range(&self, address: &str) -> String { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn fetch_range  fetches a range address string: the spanned text with phext structure bytes preserved — the covered segments re-assembled at their coordinates into a phext (chain) fragment (scroll breaks between covered scrolls inside a universe, CHAIN_BREAK between universes, never a trailing CHAIN_CLOSE: a fragment is an excerpt, not a terminated chain), so the result round-trips as a phext fragment. Empty segments contribute nothing. Any parse or resolution failure fetches the empty string, as Chain::fetch does. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment :: 2077 :: 1
