item+ function src/phext.rs :: verify_checked_scroll :: pub fn verify_checked_scroll(scroll: &str) -> bool { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn verify_checked_scroll  true when `scroll` ends with a well-formed integrity trailer whose checksum matches the payload before it. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2136 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: verify_commit :: pub fn verify_commit(input: &str, entries: &[Coordinate], commit: Coordinate) -> bool { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @fn verify_commit  true when the scroll at `commit` is a commit marker whose count matches `entries` and whose checksum matches the entries' current contents, in order. Any missing, altered, or torn entry — or a missing marker — yields false: the batch did not commit.  The document is indexed with a single `explode` pass and the marker and every entry are read from that map. The previous implementation ran the scanning `fetch` once for the marker and once per entry, so verification cost grew quadratically in document length — the cost a WAL recovery pass pays over the whole log, per transaction. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 2425 :: 1
item+ function src/phext.rs :: volume_break :: pub fn volume_break(&mut self) { :: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ @fn volume_break ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ :: doc comment :: 1708 :: 1
item+ struct src/phext.rs :: Chain :: pub struct Chain { :: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- @struct Chain  A chain: an ordered series of complete phexts within one stream, joined by CHAIN_BREAK (0x02) and optionally terminated by CHAIN_CLOSE (0x03). The chain layer is a stream-level layer, one rung above the library break: the nine dimension breaks structure a single phext, while the two chain bytes structure a stream of phexts. Each member is a whole phext, worked with the ordinary phext functions in this module; read in succession, a chain's members are editions in sequence — the chain is how a phext stream encodes time.  `closed` records how the chain ended: true when a CHAIN_CLOSE terminated it, false when the stream simply ran out (EOF). A chain that ends at EOF is unclosed — possibly truncated — and is never silently promoted to closed. A stream with no chain bytes at all is a single unclosed chain holding one member phext: every pre-0.6 phext reads this way. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- :: doc comment :: 1473 :: 1
